PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H}

The least plausible option is H. It fabricates an unsubstantiated link between the Kuji-in hand seals, Taoist “nine holes,” yin-yang erotic rituals, and supposed kunoichi perception-amplification techniques—none of which appear in any surviving shinobi literature or in the documented transmission history of the Bansenshukai. The other choices, while varying in historical likelihood, at least rest on attested practices (redaction, moral censorship, physical wear, oral mnemonics, or invisible-ink methods) or on documented political actors; H does not. 

(The original question asks only for the single least-plausible choice, so the above identifies H.)

4.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C

The consistent presence of blanks in identical positions across all surviving post-original copies rules out explanations relying on random or selective physical damage (G), pure oral-memory triggers (F), or post-copying redactions by groups like the Oniwaban that post-date the 1676 text (D). Plausible mechanisms remain those that could have operated at the moment of the first transcription from a now-lost original: moral squeamishness of copyists (B), deliberate concealment via aburidashi (E), or authorial excision (A).  

Option C is the least tenable. Lady Saigō died in 1589—nearly a century before Fujibayashi compiled the Bansenshukai—and had no documented connection to Hattori Hanzo as stepdaughter. Any “kunoichi techniques” she might have used would already have been irrelevant to an Edo-period manuscript written under a different regime; the proposed motive of protecting Tokugawa legitimacy therefore rests on anachronism and fabricated genealogy rather than historical or logical inference.}

